[Music]  hey guys  today I have a video about probably my  third favorite subject after beer and my  dog Oswald and that is squatting this  video reviews a new study that came out  called quadriceps effort during squat  exercise depends on hip extensor muscle  strategy and it is just as exciting as  it sounds it was a follow up to a 2012  study done by the same lead researcher  Megan Bryant --an and in the first study  what they did is they found the  participants maximum knee and hip  extension torque at various joint angles  and then had them work up to a 90  percent squat in increments of 10% so 50  60 70 80 90 % and then compared the  amount of torque that they would have to  produce at those joints to lift the  weight to the amount of torque that they  were capable of producing at those  joints and the resultant number was  termed relative muscular effort and that  was the minimum amount of torque  necessary to lift the weight relative to  what they were capable of and one of the  surprising findings in that study is  that even with 90 % loads at the bottom  of a squat the most challenging part for  your quads the relative muscular effort  was only about 60% and that's kind of a  puzzling finding because obviously a 90  % squat probably represents more than a  60% effort from the quads and so in this  follow-up study basically what they were  trying to do was model hamstrings  co-contraction  so the first study was looking at the  minimum amount of work the quads would  have to do to extend the knees but in  the real world they're never going to  only be doing the minimum amount of work  they would have to do because the  hamstrings will also be contracting to  extend the hips  but as they contract that resists knee  extension there they insert on the  opposite side  the joint from the quad so as the quads  are trying to extend the knees as the  hamstrings are contracting to extend the  hips that's resisting the knee extension  that the quads are that the quads are  doing so they developed two models the  first model assumed that the glutes and  hamstrings shared the load equally so if  they had to produce 80% of maximum hip  extension torque the glutes were  contracting 80% as hard as they could  and the hamstrings 80% as hard as they  could the second model assumed that the  glutes did everything they possibly  could before the hamstrings did anything  so for that same 80% contraction the  glutes who are already contracting 100%  as hard as they possibly could and the  hamstrings to make up the rest would be  50 60 % contraction and so these would  represent basically the upper and lower  bounds of how hard the hamstrings could  be working in the squat model 1 assuming  they contract equally that would be the  upper bound and model 2 assuming that  the glutes did everything they possibly  could before the hamstrings kicked in  would represent the lower bound so they  took the data from the first study and  ran it through these two models to look  and see how it would affect quadriceps  effort since the original study was only  looking at the minimum knee extension  that the minimum knee extension torque  that would have to be produced to lift  the load the second study wanted to look  and see how different hip extensor  strategies would affect the quadriceps  effort with model 1 being kind of the  most challenging you could expect it to  be for the quads and model 2 being the  least challenging you would expect it to  be for the quads so here you can see the  data that they got from the two models  the black line you see there is the data  from the original study just looking at  the external torque that would be the  minnow  amount of net me extension torque that  needed to be produced to lift the weight  so that's not taking into account  hamstrings co-contraction at all the red  line is model one that's the one that  assumed that hamstring and glute  contraction increased at the same rate  so you know for every X amount of glute  contraction you got X amount of  hamstring contraction the the same  amount relative to maximum and the Green  Line is model to assuming that the  glutes would do everything they possibly  could to create hip extension twerk  before the hamstrings kicked in as well  and so what you can see is that green  line it's it's considerably higher than  the black line the minimum amount but  they're all the way to the right side of  the graph at the bottom of the squat  knee flexion angles above a hundred and  five degrees the peak it reaches is  eighty seven percent of the maximum  amount of force the quads could produce  so this is a 90 percent squat so eighty  seven percent of the maximum amount of  work the quads could do that's pretty  darn close to what you'd expect the red  line on the other hand assuming  equivalent amount of glute and hamstring  action to produce the required hip  extension to work you see there at the  bottom of the squat it goes all the way  up to a hundred and twenty percent  basically showing that if that model was  true or close to how reality actually  works the the quads would have to be  essentially a fifth stronger than they  actually are twenty percent stronger  than they actually are  to create the knee extension torque  required to to lift the weight to get  the weight moving out of the hole so  basically what we can take from this is  that model to the one assumed  that basically the the hamstrings we're  doing as little as they possibly could  and the glutes doing as much as they  possibly could to create the required  hip extension to work is probably pretty  close to how the squat actually works in  reality and that kind of makes sense if  you think about it intuitively because  basically the quads specifically the the  vast eye excluding the rectus femoris  since it's actually a hip flexor but the  other three quad muscles and the glutes  they're single joint muscles they the  quads create knee extension torque and  the glutes create hip extension to work  without really screwing with the other  joint very much whereas the hamstrings  while they are powerful hip extension  extensors they also resist the quads  because they create a knee flexion  torque basically resisting what the  quads are trying to do so it kind of  makes sense that your central nervous  system wouldn't activate them more than  more than it actually had to to create  the required hip extension torque to  complete the movement because the more  active they are at the harder the  movement becomes for your quads so what  do we do with all of this there are two  major takeaways the first is that you  shouldn't really be trying to prioritize  your hamstrings in the squat the squat  is is a pretty crummy hamstrings  exercise anyways it's it's very  overrated in that regard I've written  about that a fair amount in the past and  articles will be linked in the  description of this video going into  that in more detail but if you try to  squat in a manner that maximizes  hamstrings involvement sitting back more  and leaning forward quite a bit more  what that's actually going to do is it's  going to make the movement quite a bit  harder for your quads coming out of the  hole while also again sitting back more  increasing hip extension demands so  there's really no upside that you get  from that  the second big implication is that if  you do have issues coming out of the  hole in the squat if you if your butt  shoots up and you wind up in more of a  good morning position something that can  help with that is obviously  strengthening your quads so they can do  more of the work but a secondary thing  would be to work on strengthening your  glutes because essentially the the more  they can do the less your hamstrings  have to do it kicks it pushes back to  the point that your hamstrings are  having to contract harder and harder and  harder to produce the required hip  extension torque so if you are finding  yourself in a good morning squat  position and you do have strong quads  the next place you should look to  rectify that would be your glutes if  they're not very strong then even if you  do have strong quads you could still be  finding yourself in that position all  right guys that's about it for this  video this this video was a somewhat  abbreviated version of an article I  recently wrote for my website to string  theory calm that will be linked in the  description along with the study that I  was reviewing and so if this is a topic  that interests you or if I said  something that that didn't sound quite  right rest assured that I probably went  into that subject and quite a bit more  depth in the article itself so go ahead  and check that out if this is if this is  a subject that interests you and other  than that thanks for watching please  like and subscribe and I'll see you in  the next video

